

Chronos‑State Bifurcation Engine: A Unified Architecture for Multi‑Timeline Planetary Simulation

Author: C. Matthew BlackwellBlackwell Research Labs, Lafayette, Louisiana

Abstract

Planetary simulations traditionally operate within deterministic or ensemble‑based frameworks that do not capture chaotic bifurcation or multi‑timeline divergence. This manuscript introduces the Chronos‑State Bifurcation Engine, a unified architecture integrating planetary physics solvers, cryptographic provenance, image‑derived state‑space initialization, hyper‑chaotic evolution, and dual‑timeline validation. The system enables physically constrained exploration of alternate planetary futures through bifurcation mapping, attractor‑basin analysis, and SSIM‑based plausibility filtering. Results demonstrate that controlled chaotic perturbations can produce distinct yet physically valid future states of planetary systems. This architecture represents a new class of simulation framework with implications for planetary science, chaos theory, and high‑performance computational modeling.

1. Introduction

Planetary systems exhibit complex dynamical behavior driven by fluid motion, magnetospheric interactions, gravitational coupling, and radiative processes. Traditional simulation frameworks model these systems using deterministic solvers or ensemble‑based uncertainty propagation. While effective for forecasting and analysis, these approaches do not capture the full spectrum of chaotic divergence inherent in nonlinear planetary environments.

Chaos theory provides mathematical tools for understanding sensitivity to initial conditions, but its integration into planetary simulation remains limited. Likewise, cryptographic provenance systems ensure data integrity in mission telemetry, yet they are rarely incorporated into simulation pipelines. The Chronos‑State Bifurcation Engine unifies these domains into a single architecture capable of exploring physically plausible alternate futures.

This manuscript presents the design, methodology, and results of the Chronos‑State Engine, demonstrating its ability to generate bifurcated timelines, map attractor basins, and validate physical plausibility using structural similarity metrics.

2. Background

